      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm studying for my clinical boards and trying to get a better handle on dermatology. i noticed some of my study guides focus a lot on how things look on light skin. can u explain how the presentation of melanoma might differ across different racial or skin pigment groups? like, what should i be looking for in patients with darker skin tones?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Melanoma can present differently on darker skin tones, often mimicking benign nevi, which can lead to delayed diagnosis. Instead of the classic asymmetric, multicolored lesions seen on lighter skin, melanoma on darker skin may appear as a dusky, brown, black, or reddish lesion. Pay close attention to changes in existing moles, looking for growth, color variation within the dark background, or symmetry changes. Early and thorough examination is crucial, which sometimes requires specialized tools to assess subtle changes.",
